on how you can become successful

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:26.700
in the first years of your career,

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:27.770
so have a look here as well.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:30.250
In this video, we are going to
proceed in the following way.

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:32.890
First, I'm going to
introduce some key concepts

00:01:32.890 --> 00:01:35.070
and metrics that I believe
you need to understand

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:37.640
and you need to master in order
to get your finances right.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:38.840
Second, I'm going to share

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:41.140
with you one crucial major mistake

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:44.280
that I believe especially the
population of Germany is doing

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:45.123
with belong to their finances

00:01:45.123 --> 00:01:47.640
and I want to help you avoid this mistake

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:50.640
so you can get your finances
right from the very beginning.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.340
Third, I'm going to give you
some very actionable advice

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:56.300
from my experiences on what
you can do to get started

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.520
to start investing.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.910
This will enable you to get
started without the need

00:01:59.910 --> 00:02:02.400
of doing months and months
of research and readings.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:03.860
Let's start with the first block,

00:02:03.860 --> 00:02:06.170
and this is about metrics and concepts.

00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:08.520
I remember that when I was
a rookie with McKinsey,

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.620
one of the partners told me in the context

00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:11.810
of a study engagement,

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:14.450
you can't manage what you don't measure.

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:16.850
So before you really start
investing time with something,

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:18.860
before you really start
optimizing something,

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:21.450
you should establish
and define some metrics

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:23.610
that you will use to measure your progress

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:26.440
and your achievement towards
a specific goal that you have.

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:28.300
And I believe that this is very much true

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:30.270
with your personal finances as well.

00:02:30.270 --> 00:02:32.660
You need one metric that you optimize.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:34.540
And in the personal finance literature,

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:36.900
the metric that is most
often cited and referred

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.780
to is the metric of net worth.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:41.830
Even though it simplifies
a couple of things

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:44.470
if you're only looking at
this one thing I do believe

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.240
that if you optimize your net worth,

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.760
if you make sure that your net worth

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:49.490
steadily increases over time,

00:02:49.490 --> 00:02:51.930
this will solve pretty much
all your financial problems

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:53.200
for you that you will have.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.480
So how exactly is the net worth defined?

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:58.560
How can you calculate
your personal net worth?

00:02:58.560 --> 00:03:00.720
And it's much more easy than you think.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.870
The net worth is measured

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:04.890
as your assets minus your liabilities.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:06.630
Assets minus liabilities.

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:09.180
So what exactly do I mean
when I refer to assets

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.760
or liabilities in a private context?

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:14.240
To illustrate what
assets and liabilities mean

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.280
for the typical private
citizen, I adopted a report

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:18.740
from the Deutsche Bundesbank

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:20.870
where they show a stylized balance sheet

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:22.520
of a private person in Germany.

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:25.050
What does this private
balance sheet consist of?

00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:26.880
On the asset side of the balance sheet,

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.760
you can distinguish tangible assets
from the financial assets.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.120
Typical tangible assets
would be Real Estate

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.810
so all the houses or apartments

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:35.420
that you might own as a private person.

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:38.270
Also, if you're the direct owner
of a commercial enterprise,

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:40.120
then the net value of the enterprise,

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:42.100
you can also consider a tangible asset.

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:44.340
In addition to that, it can
be all kinds of other things

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:45.980
of tangible value that you own.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.120
For instance, jewellery,
vehicles, art collections

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:50.630
or anything similar to that.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.110
Looking at the financial assets,

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:54.610
private persons can have a
checking or a savings account,

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:57.090
so pretty much the money
that you have in the bank.

00:03:57.090 --> 00:03:59.160
Then you can have stocks,
bonds, derivatives,

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.880
or other financial products like that.

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:03.440
And especially in Germany,
many people have a balance

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.160
from a pension or life insurance.

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.840
That of course has a significant
intrinsic value as well.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:10.190
And especially in Germany,
many people have a balance

00:04:10.190 --> 00:04:11.990
from a pension or life insurance,

00:04:11.990 --> 00:04:13.869
which can of course hold a
significant intrinsic value

00:04:13.869 --> 00:04:14.770
as well.

00:04:14.770 --> 00:04:16.240
And of course there can be all kinds

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:18.640
of other financial holdings
that people might own.

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:21.470
They can be subsumed under the
term of financial assets.

00:04:21.470 --> 00:04:23.850
What are typical
liabilities that people hold

00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:24.780
on a personal level?

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.730
One of the most common one are mortgages.

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:29.130
So if you buy a home and
you get a loan from that

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:30.940
from the bank, and then
you have a mortgage

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:32.890
on that house, there
will be a mortgage credit

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:34.060
that would be liability.

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.790
What unfortunately also many
people have is consumer credit.

00:04:36.790 --> 00:04:39.510
So this will be open
balances on your credit card

00:04:39.510 --> 00:04:41.540
or other lines of credits that you use

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:43.280
to finance consumer spending.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:45.020
So if you buy a television at the store

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.830
and you agree to not pay it right now,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:48.750
but at some point later in the future,

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.010
this would be an example
of consumer credit.

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:52.930
Another category would be student loans.

00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:55.470
So if you incur debt
to study at university

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:57.060
and finally business credits,

00:04:57.060 --> 00:04:59.340
so if you take a credit
on your personal name

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.720
and all to finance some kind

00:05:00.720 --> 00:05:03.060
of commercial business
operation that you are running.

00:05:03.060 --> 00:05:04.910
And this is now how you can calculate

00:05:04.910 --> 00:05:06.320
your personal net worth.

00:05:06.320 --> 00:05:08.290
So if you sum up the
value of all your assets

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:10.240
and you deduct all your liabilities,

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:12.780
then the result of that is the net worth.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.630
So to put a more
intuitive meaning to that,

00:05:14.630 --> 00:05:17.620
the net worth is the value
of the funds available to you

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:21.080
in the longterm to finance
your private investing

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:22.280
and spending activities.

00:05:22.280 --> 00:05:24.320
So your net worth can be negative

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:26.137
if the liabilities exceed your assets,

00:05:26.137 --> 00:05:28.140
you have a negative net worth.

00:05:28.140 --> 00:05:29.790
But of course what you want to aim for is

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:31.610
to have a high positive net worth.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.770
So to own assets that
significantly exceed the value

00:05:34.770 --> 00:05:36.690
of all the liabilities that you have.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:39.530
Why do so many young
professionals struggle

00:05:39.530 --> 00:05:41.010
to increase the net worth?

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:42.640
And at least from my observations,

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:44.870
the typical career path
of a young professional is

00:05:44.870 --> 00:05:47.300
that he spent several years at university.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:49.680
During university many of
these people had a rather

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:50.980
frugal lifestyle.

00:05:50.980 --> 00:05:53.330
They lived in rather small shared flats,

00:05:53.330 --> 00:05:55.680
they weren't able to
afford too many luxuries

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:58.580
and then suddenly after
university they get the first job,

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.760
maybe it's already the
second or the third job,

00:06:00.760 --> 00:06:03.430
and they realize that now
they have a significant income

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:04.470
at their disposal.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:07.130
At least the income is much
more significant than it was

00:06:07.130 --> 00:06:08.880
when they were a university student.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:10.940
And they realize that
with this higher income,

00:06:10.940 --> 00:06:13.440
their financial means
increased significantly

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.110
and they start to improve the lifestyle.

00:06:15.110 --> 00:06:16.960
They start to move into a larger flat,

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:19.040
they start to get a nicer vehicle.

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.140
They get some nice cloth,
some other luxury equipment

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:23.360
an expensive laptop.

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.410
So they increase their
spending just to the level

00:06:25.410 --> 00:06:27.570
that they can afford with
their current salary.

00:06:27.570 --> 00:06:29.420
So what happens if they get a promotion

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:30.770
and earn even more money,

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:33.630
where then this offer means
to getting even a larger flat,

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:36.380
even a larger car, even
more expensive cloth

00:06:36.380 --> 00:06:37.500
and so on and so on.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.890
This pattern of behavior is
called lifestyle inflation.

00:06:40.890 --> 00:06:42.310
And this leads to a phenomenon

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:44.970
that is called high-income low net worth.

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:46.550
Even if you're in the top 10%

00:06:46.550 --> 00:06:50.460
or maybe even top 1% of
income earners in your country,

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:53.060
if you spend it all, if
you do not save anything

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:56.010
and the only thing you
buy are consumer goods,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:58.720
then you will barely build
any net worth at all.

00:06:58.720 --> 00:07:00.610
So this can make you end up in a situation

00:07:00.610 --> 00:07:02.740
that even though you
earn significant amounts

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:04.610
of money year after year,

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:07.090
your net worth is barely larger than zero

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:08.860
because you didn't save anything

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:11.130
and you did not do any investment.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:14.180
So let's assume now that so
far we are on the same page.

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:15.330
Either I convinced you now

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:16.870
or maybe you already had the attitude

00:07:16.870 --> 00:07:19.230
that yes, you need to
save a certain amount

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.090
of your income and you need to invest it.

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:22.780
But now you might ask yourself, Heinrich,

00:07:22.780 --> 00:07:24.710
how should I invest my money?

00:07:24.710 --> 00:07:27.230
I mean currently the interest
rates are pretty much zero,

00:07:27.230 --> 00:07:29.440
if I just leave my money
on my account in the bank,

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:31.330
the inflation rate, just eat it up.

00:07:31.330 --> 00:07:32.700
What shall I do with my money?

00:07:32.700 --> 00:07:35.390
Let's start by looking into
what the average person

00:07:35.390 --> 00:07:36.860
is actually doing with this money.

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:39.420
And in this video I'm going to
especially reference numbers

00:07:39.420 --> 00:07:40.960
and statistics from Germany.

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:43.660
So apologies of this might be
a bit less interesting to you

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:45.870
if you're not a German
or not living in Germany,

00:07:45.870 --> 00:07:48.170
but I'm sure that the
conclusions that are drawn

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:50.480
from the numbers are
applicable independent

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:51.510
from where you live.

00:07:51.510 --> 00:07:54.670
So as you can see here,
Germans hold the vast majority

00:07:54.670 --> 00:07:57.610
of their assets in cash or bank deposits,

00:07:57.610 --> 00:07:59.170
so they just leave it on the bank,

00:07:59.170 --> 00:08:01.130
they just save in as your grandma did,

00:08:01.130 --> 00:08:04.080
they just prefer to have
it under their pillow.

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:06.270
Then closely after that, are insurances,

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.370
and this is again a very German thing,

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:10.730
Germans like to buy pension insurances

00:08:10.730 --> 00:08:13.960
or life insurances and they
invest a significant amount

00:08:13.960 --> 00:08:16.420
of money in these insurance plans.

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:17.690
In this video, I'm not going

00:08:17.690 --> 00:08:19.400
to talk about insurances too much,

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:21.450
I just want to mention the main downside

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.087
of many of these insurance plans

00:08:23.087 --> 00:08:25.210
and this is that they cost a lot of money.

00:08:25.210 --> 00:08:27.090
The insurance provider would take away

00:08:27.090 --> 00:08:29.240
significant amount of all the money

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:31.910
that you pay in as costs
for its own operations.

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:34.410
So very frequently the
payoffs that you get

00:08:34.410 --> 00:08:35.880
from these insurance plans

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.760
are not really worth the investment.

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:39.520
And of course this is
now a generous statement

00:08:39.520 --> 00:08:41.510
and you would need to look
into the individual plans

00:08:41.510 --> 00:08:43.340
to understand whether
it makes sense or not.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:46.170
The next asset class is
stocks or funds of stocks,

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:48.930
and this is where really
wanna put your attention on

00:08:48.930 --> 00:08:51.310
because stocks play only a minor role

00:08:51.310 --> 00:08:55.130
in the asset allocation of the
average John Doe in Germany.

00:08:55.130 --> 00:08:56.440
And you might tell yourself, no way,

00:08:56.440 --> 00:08:59.260
of course stocks are risky,
who buys stocks anyway,

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:01.960
but what is especially
interesting in my perspective is

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:05.160
that Germany here really
has a special role compared

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:06.050
to other countries.

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:08.590
These numbers are from a report of 2018

00:09:08.590 --> 00:09:10.780
and they show the share of the population

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:12.270
that is holding stocks.

00:09:12.270 --> 00:09:15.010
And you see that why in Germany only 6%

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.030
of the population is holding stocks.

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.600
In our neighbor country, France 15%

00:09:19.600 --> 00:09:21.240
of the population is owning stocks,

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:25.090
in the US, 25% of the
population is owning stocks

00:09:25.090 --> 00:09:27.530
and in the Netherlands 30%

00:09:27.530 --> 00:09:29.410
of the population is owning stocks.

00:09:29.410 --> 00:09:31.240
And this is really
interesting and surprising.

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:33.430
And also in Academia,
there's lots of research

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.870
in the personal finance field
looking at these questions,

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:39.320
why are only so few Germans
investing in the stock market?

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:41.730
While all kinds of
reasons are hypothesized,

00:09:41.730 --> 00:09:42.730
the result is clear

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:45.630
and this is that the Germans
lose a significant opportunity

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:48.260
for wealth creation because only so few

00:09:48.260 --> 00:09:50.680
of them actually invest
in the stock market.

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:52.180
But probably a significant share

00:09:52.180 --> 00:09:53.490
of you already understood this

00:09:53.490 --> 00:09:55.570
because yesterday I did a little survey

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:58.440
on my Instagram Channel
@firmlearning and I asked who

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.740
of you already invest in stock regularly?

00:10:00.740 --> 00:10:03.150
And I was happy to see
that a significant portion

00:10:03.150 --> 00:10:05.690
of you is actually are
already investing regularly

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:06.523
in stocks.

00:10:06.523 --> 00:10:07.970
So very glad that here at Firm Learning

00:10:07.970 --> 00:10:10.570
we already have a pretty
financially savvy community.

00:10:10.570 --> 00:10:12.140
And the reason for that is that stocks,

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:15.320
at least in the longterm,
almost always outperform other

00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:16.153
asset classes.

00:10:16.153 --> 00:10:18.960
Here is your comparison
of annualized results

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:21.800
of different asset classes
over the last 15 years

00:10:21.800 --> 00:10:23.690
and you see that Large Cap Stocks,

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:26.510
so stocks of large companies have achieved

00:10:26.510 --> 00:10:29.140
annualized returns of
~8% so they're increasing

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:31.910
by ~8% every year on average.

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:34.210
And yes, while it is
true that the volatility,

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:36.280
so the risk of this
investment is quite high

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:38.100
so if you look at it month after month

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:40.560
or year after year there
can be quite significant

00:10:40.560 --> 00:10:43.570
fluctuations and there also
can be significant losses

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.810
as we also see right now at the moment

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:47.550
in the current financial crisis

00:10:47.550 --> 00:10:49.030
if your stocks in the long term,

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:50.480
at least when you look at the past,

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.100
you are always on the
winning side in the variant.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:54.980
In the personal finance literature,

00:10:54.980 --> 00:10:57.190
there's a broad consensus that investing

00:10:57.190 --> 00:11:00.960
in stocks is a good financial
decision for most people.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:03.050
So this would be my
first key recommendation.

00:11:03.050 --> 00:11:04.390
If you're a young professional

00:11:04.390 --> 00:11:07.230
and you are able to save a
bit of money to the side,

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:08.980
start investing in the stock market

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:10.680
and in a few moments I'm going to show you

00:11:10.680 --> 00:11:13.170
what I exactly bought, how I got started,

00:11:13.170 --> 00:11:15.840
what I believed at the time
from doing the research,

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.560
what the good first investments

00:11:17.560 --> 00:11:18.810
in the stock market would be.

00:11:18.810 --> 00:11:21.040
But before I get there, I
just want to stress the point

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:24.920
that I believe that it is
really crucial to start early.

00:11:24.920 --> 00:11:27.660
This is another point that I
frequently hear from talking

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:30.110
to friends and other peers
and this is the day say,

00:11:30.110 --> 00:11:33.240
well, I'm in my late twenties
I'm in my early thirties,

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:37.400
I'm just so young, I just do
not need to save money yet.

00:11:37.400 --> 00:11:38.960
Right now I want to enjoy life.

00:11:38.960 --> 00:11:41.040
I want to get the best life possible

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.940
and the maybe later some
years down the line,

00:11:43.940 --> 00:11:47.030
I'm going to start saving
and to think about my future.

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:49.080
And from my opinion at least if you want

00:11:49.080 --> 00:11:52.410
to optimize your net worth,
this is a significant mistake.

00:11:52.410 --> 00:11:55.000
These people tend to
significantly underestimate

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.300
how money can compound over
time and the advantages

00:11:58.300 --> 00:12:00.460
that you have if you
start investing early.

00:12:00.460 --> 00:12:02.050
And here's let's have a simple chart

00:12:02.050 --> 00:12:04.050
that I found online
that compares two types

00:12:04.050 --> 00:12:05.320
of investment strategies.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:06.670
The one guy on the left starts

00:12:06.670 --> 00:12:10.650
to invest $2,000 every
year, starting when he's 19

00:12:10.650 --> 00:12:12.070
until when he is 26

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:14.740
and will not invest any
more money afterwards.

00:12:14.740 --> 00:12:17.010
The guy on the right is not
investing any money at all

00:12:17.010 --> 00:12:20.890
when he's young, but only starts
investing €2000 every year

00:12:20.890 --> 00:12:22.790
from the year of 27.

00:12:22.790 --> 00:12:24.260
And if you compare now the value

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:26.610
of their holdings at the age of 65,

00:12:26.610 --> 00:12:29.430
you see that the left guy has
a significant higher value

00:12:29.430 --> 00:12:31.060
in his holding than the guy on the right,

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:34.360
even though he put into
significantly less money

00:12:34.360 --> 00:12:35.790
than the person on the right.

00:12:35.790 --> 00:12:38.230
And in this calculation
exercise, they calculated

00:12:38.230 --> 00:12:40.890
with 10% return year on year.

00:12:40.890 --> 00:12:43.270
So you could argue non
that 10% in the longterm

00:12:43.270 --> 00:12:45.480
are a bit too high of a return rate.

00:12:45.480 --> 00:12:47.410
And of course, if you
calculate with a lower rate

00:12:47.410 --> 00:12:48.820
of return then the differences

00:12:48.820 --> 00:12:50.830
between these two
examples really diminish,

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:52.020
but I hope that you get the point

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:54.980
that starting early is extremely important

00:12:54.980 --> 00:12:57.790
and can deliver significant
amounts of value to you

00:12:57.790 --> 00:12:58.980
and to your net worth.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:00.960
So what can you do now to get started?

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:03.500
And one thing that I know
many people are afraid of

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:06.500
is the pressure to needing
to select the right stocks.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:08.780
How do you know what stocks will increase

00:13:08.780 --> 00:13:10.170
in value over time?

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:11.640
And the good message that I have

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.510
for you here is that it's much easier.

00:13:13.510 --> 00:13:16.350
It's not about picking the
one, two, or three top stocks,

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:17.850
but you can go broad.

00:13:17.850 --> 00:13:19.780
And the main reason for that is the effect

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:21.020
of diversification.

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:22.300
So when you look at risk,

00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:25.100
you can often distinguish
the systematic risk

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:26.830
from the unsystematic risk.

00:13:26.830 --> 00:13:30.300
So the systematic risk is the
risk that affects all stocks

00:13:30.300 --> 00:13:31.220
at the same time.

00:13:31.220 --> 00:13:33.470
So it's something like the
current health situation

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:35.900
affecting pretty much
all stocks out there,

00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:38.800
it is not a risk that is
immanent on a specific stock.

00:13:38.800 --> 00:13:41.110
Different from that, is
the unsystematic risk.

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:44.300
And this is the risk that is
immanent in a specific company.

00:13:44.300 --> 00:13:46.910
So it's a risk that only
this one company is facing.

00:13:46.910 --> 00:13:49.180
For instance, the risk
that a specific plant

00:13:49.180 --> 00:13:52.160
of a company needs to shut
down due to an emergency event

00:13:52.160 --> 00:13:54.160
and then the company
cannot produce anymore.

00:13:54.160 --> 00:13:56.900
And this risk can be
reduced by diversifying.

00:13:56.900 --> 00:14:00.200
So by owning several of
these stocks, not only one.

00:14:00.200 --> 00:14:01.980
And as you can see here in the chart,

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:05.260
the more stocks you own, the
lower this unsystematic risk

00:14:05.260 --> 00:14:06.093
that you hold.

00:14:06.093 --> 00:14:08.440
But of course at some point
there is a diminishing effect,

00:14:08.440 --> 00:14:10.400
so after certain number of stocks,

00:14:10.400 --> 00:14:13.040
the risk goes only down
by a marginal amount

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:14.680
for the next best stock that you buy.

00:14:14.680 --> 00:14:16.960
Most people see this inflection point

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:18.710
from where then the additional benefit

00:14:18.710 --> 00:14:20.620
of further stocks reduces more

00:14:20.620 --> 00:14:22.420
and more to be around 20 stocks.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:25.220
So this means that we should
own at least 20 stocks

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:28.690
in our portfolio to get the
effect of diversification.

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:31.400
So what is the portfolio of
stocks that you should buy?

00:14:31.400 --> 00:14:33.020
And financial theory tells us

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:35.520
that we should own the market portfolio,

00:14:35.520 --> 00:14:37.320
but what is the market portfolio?

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:39.410
The definition of a market portfolio is

00:14:39.410 --> 00:14:43.970
"a portfolio consisting of the
weighted sum of every asset

00:14:43.970 --> 00:14:46.380
in the market with
weights in the proportion

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:48.030
that they exist in the market".

00:14:48.030 --> 00:14:51.970
So the market portfolio means
to pretty much own every asset

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:53.920
that exists out there in the market.

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:56.430
So the reason why you should
own the market portfolio

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.540
compared to any other portfolio out there,

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:01.250
probably is material enough
for a whole other video.

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:03.740
So please just let us
assume here that it is right

00:15:03.740 --> 00:15:05.690
that you want to own the market portfolio.

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:08.150
So you might ask yourself
now, well, how do I do that?

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:10.810
Because obviously I cannot
buy every single stock

00:15:10.810 --> 00:15:11.770
that exists out there.

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:15.170
I cannot own every single
asset that exists in the world.

00:15:15.170 --> 00:15:17.570
And because this is true, we need a proxy

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:18.840
for the market portfolio.

00:15:18.840 --> 00:15:21.630
Something that comes close
enough to the market portfolio

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:24.820
and that is set up in a way
that we can easily invest in it.

00:15:24.820 --> 00:15:26.320
And typically you do that by investing

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:28.690
in so-called exchange-traded funds (ETFs).

00:15:28.690 --> 00:15:31.150
So in a simplified way, these are baskets

00:15:31.150 --> 00:15:34.310
of all kinds of stocks that
they put together according

00:15:34.310 --> 00:15:37.590
to certain criteria to
enable you to easily invest

00:15:37.590 --> 00:15:39.180
in such a market portfolio.

00:15:39.180 --> 00:15:40.570
And if you ask yourself now, Heinrich,

00:15:40.570 --> 00:15:42.550
there are so many funds out there,

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:45.030
which specific funds shall I buy?

00:15:45.030 --> 00:15:47.510
Then if you look into the
personal finance literature,

00:15:47.510 --> 00:15:49.610
while there are of course many,
many different options

00:15:49.610 --> 00:15:51.780
of combinations of funds you could buy

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:53.700
to build such a market portfolio,

00:15:53.700 --> 00:15:56.850
the most easiest combination
that comes close enough,

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.470
if you just want something
easy to get you started,

00:15:59.470 --> 00:16:02.910
it would be a combination
of 70% of the portfolio

00:16:02.910 --> 00:16:07.910
in the MSCI World and 30% of
the MSCI Emerging Markets.

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:10.950
So the MSCI World and
the MSCI Emerging Markets

00:16:10.950 --> 00:16:14.650
are two indices that are
brought out by the company MSCI.

00:16:14.650 --> 00:16:17.950
There are lots of ETFs that try
to replicate the performance

00:16:17.950 --> 00:16:21.160
of these indices that you can
buy in order to participate

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:24.480
from the development of this
index for your own portfolio.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.670
So what are these two indices composed of?

00:16:26.670 --> 00:16:29.300
So let's start with the
first with the MSCI World.

00:16:29.300 --> 00:16:31.990
And the MSCI World tries to
give you a good combination

00:16:31.990 --> 00:16:34.900
of large companies in developed countries.

00:16:34.900 --> 00:16:37.100
The top 10 companies that are represented

00:16:37.100 --> 00:16:40.940
in this index are Microsoft,
Apple, Amazon, Facebook,

00:16:40.940 --> 00:16:44.270
two types of Alphabet, meaning
Google, Johnson &amp;Johnson,

00:16:44.270 --> 00:16:46.500
Nestle, JPMorgan Chase, and Visa.

00:16:46.500 --> 00:16:49.820
In total, these 10
companies make about 15%

00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:50.940
of the total holdings.

00:16:50.940 --> 00:16:52.970
You can see that there are
many, many more companies

00:16:52.970 --> 00:16:55.670
that the MSCI Word has
represented in his index.

00:16:55.670 --> 00:16:57.390
If you now look into the country split,

00:16:57.390 --> 00:17:00.350
the five most commonly
represented countries are the US

00:17:00.350 --> 00:17:05.350
with 64%, Japan with 9%, the
UK with 5%, France with 4%,

00:17:07.070 --> 00:17:10.160
Switzerland with 3% and roughly 15%

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:11.540
of other countries in it.

00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:14.700
Let's now compare this to the
MSCI Emerging Markets index,

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:16.670
which tries to give you a combination

00:17:16.670 --> 00:17:18.660
of larger companies of countries

00:17:18.660 --> 00:17:20.310
which are still in the growth phase.

00:17:20.310 --> 00:17:22.320
And the top 10 companies represented

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:25.870
in the MSCI Emerging market
index is first Alibaba,

00:17:25.870 --> 00:17:29.560
then Tencent, Taiwan
Semiconductors, Samsung Electronics,

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:32.700
China Construction,
Naspers, Ping An Insurance,

00:17:32.700 --> 00:17:35.810
China Mobile, ICBC and Reliance Industries.

00:17:35.810 --> 00:17:39.200
And together these top
10 companies make up 28%

00:17:39.200 --> 00:17:40.210
of the total index.

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:41.290
So you can see that here as well,

00:17:41.290 --> 00:17:43.707
there are many other
companies represented.

00:17:43.707 --> 00:17:44.670
And as well let's look

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:46.830
into the top five represented countries.

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:51.710
Largest one is China with 41%
followed by Taiwan with 12%,

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:56.640
South Korea with 12%, India
with 8%, Brazil with 5%

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:59.400
and then 23% with other countries.

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:00.480
So here you have it guys,

00:18:00.480 --> 00:18:02.360
if you want to build a simple version

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:05.100
of a market portfolio, invest with 70%

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:07.540
of your funds into the MSCI World index

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:09.320
and with 30% of your funds

00:18:09.320 --> 00:18:11.470
into the MSCI Emerging Markets index.

00:18:11.470 --> 00:18:14.690
And if you ask yourself what
specific ETFs you should buy,

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:17.070
if you just google ETF MSCI World

00:18:17.070 --> 00:18:19.970
or ETF MSCI Emerging
Markets, you will find lots

00:18:19.970 --> 00:18:22.207
of comparisons of all the
different ETFs out there.

00:18:22.207 --> 00:18:24.580
And trust you can come to
your own conclusions here,

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:26.630
which specific product you want to buy.

00:18:26.630 --> 00:18:29.230
And again, of course it can
get much more sophisticated,

00:18:29.230 --> 00:18:31.550
you can buy all kinds of
other combinations of assets

00:18:31.550 --> 00:18:33.930
and of course you need to
realize that with this version

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:36.660
of the market portfolio,
you only invest in stocks.

00:18:36.660 --> 00:18:38.990
So maybe at some point you
might also ask yourself

00:18:38.990 --> 00:18:41.560
to add some bonds or
some other asset classes,

00:18:41.560 --> 00:18:44.050
but if you just want to get
started investing your money,

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:46.430
so for myself, I concluded
this would be a good

00:18:46.430 --> 00:18:49.010
and great start to get
my investing started

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:50.990
and now reflecting on that, looking back,

00:18:50.990 --> 00:18:54.070
I at least the believe that
this is the right way to go

00:18:54.070 --> 00:18:56.220
for at least most young
professionals out there.

00:18:56.220 --> 00:18:58.010
So again, I'm not a finance professional,

00:18:58.010 --> 00:18:59.950
I do not want to give
you investment advice.

00:18:59.950 --> 00:19:02.940
All of those are just my
personal private experiences

00:19:02.940 --> 00:19:05.270
and reflections on how
to handle your money

00:19:05.270 --> 00:19:06.450
as a young professional.

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:09.030
But I'd love to hear from you
now, what is your perspective?

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:10.880
Do you save, do you invest in stocks?

00:19:10.880 --> 00:19:13.610
How do you handle your finances
as young professionals?

